            {
                id grammar-diagnostics.include-resolution
                title "Decide what INCLUDE path resolution should be now the top-level grammar has a real name: whether the working-directory fallback survives as a second-chance lookup, and whether golden.rs still needs its chdir"
                priority 3
                kind @fix
                deps (grammar-diagnostics.spans)
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-15T17:15:47.003588Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Split out of grammar-diagnostics.spans, NOT done there. Naming the top-level source (diagnostics.source-named) incidentally changed relative INCLUDE resolution: `parse_include` resolves against `ux_dirname(fname)`, and the top-level fname was the `<utf8-memory>` placeholder, whose dirname is `./` — so a top-level relative INCLUDE resolved against the process CWD while every NESTED include already resolved against its including file's directory. Giving the parse the real path makes the top level behave like the rest. That much is a strict widening (the directory-relative path is tried first, `parse_include`'s existing CWD-relative fallback catches everything that worked before) and is verified: `cg-comp test/T_Include/grammar.cg3` from the repo root failed before and works now. What is left, and is a real behaviour change rather than a widening: (a) whether the CWD fallback should exist at all now that it is a second chance rather than the only chance; (b) whether tests/golden.rs still needs to `current_dir` into each fixture directory — it no longer needs to for includes, but the fixtures use relative paths for other things too. Neither is urgent and neither should ride along with a diagnostics change."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-09-14T13:22:37.826278Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-09-14T13:25:08.89738Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @decision
                        text "KEEP the CWD fallback, narrowed; golden.rs KEEPS its chdir but its stated reason was wrong.\n\nThe record needed correcting first. Both the decision doc and this node said the fallback is what \"catches everything that worked before\" the naming change. True but vacuous: before parse_grammar_named the top-level fname was always the memory placeholder, so ux_dirname gave \"./\" and the PRIMARY lookup was already the CWD lookup. The fallback preserved nothing. Giving the grammar a real name is what made the fallback meaningfully live for the first time.\n\nEvidence permits removal but does not compel it. docs/spec/port/src/TextualParser.md:431-437 transcribes the C++ INCLUDE branch as a single dir-relative stat that bails on failure — no second attempt — and no comment or commit ever claimed the fallback as parity. But the C++ tree is not checked out, \"the spec summary omits it\" is doing all the work, and the widened behaviour shipped in 0.4.0. So: narrow rather than delete.\n\nTwo defects fixed, neither of which anyone could be relying on. (1) The retry used the RAW token, so after a ~ expansion it retried a literal-tilde path that cannot exist, and for an absolute include it duplicated the syscall that just failed; it now retries the shell-expanded name. (2) When ux_dirname(fname) == \"./\" the two spellings name one file (the crate's own test asserts ux_dirname(\"foo.txt\") == \"./\"), so the retry is skipped there. What remains is exactly one live case: a relative include under a dir-addressed grammar, absent from that directory and present in the CWD.\n\nThe fallback now carries the comment it never had, marking it a port widening rather than parity. Pinned by include_prefers_the_including_dir_then_falls_back_to_cwd, which covers both halves — a CWD-only include resolving, and a file present in BOTH places where the neighbour must win. Nothing else in the suite would notice if the fallback broke; embed_diagnostics' relative-include test proves the primary, not the fallback.\n\ngolden.rs keeps its per-child current_dir, but not for includes: the grammar and input are passed as bare names and expected.txt is read from the dir. Only T_Include uses INCLUDE at all and it resolves from anywhere now. Comment corrected.\n\nNot done, deliberately: IncludeUnreadable still names only the primary path. Now that the fallback is a genuinely distinct lookup, naming both would be better, but that is an error-type change and not what this node is about."
                    }
                )
            }
        )
    }

