            {
                id runtime-diagnostics.rt-input-reachable
                title "Establish whether a runtime input failure is reachable at all: every stream applicator calls add_tag with an empty TagType, which cannot fail, so decide if the RT INPUT label is dead in the port and in the C++ both, and either reach it from a CLI test or retire it"
                priority 2
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-09-14T13:31:23.170346Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-09-14T13:32:26.385878Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @decision
                        text "REACHABLE — tested from a CLI, not retired. The earlier guess that it was dead was wrong, and wrong for an instructive reason: it reasoned from the applicator's add_tag callers, all of which pass TagType::empty(), and concluded nothing could take the varstring branch. What it missed is that the branch is reached from MATCHING, not from tag construction — the stream reader tests the delimiter set against each cohort as it reads (run_grammar.rs:667/693/736), a varstring tag can legally sit in DELIMITERS, and the first windows are buffered before any rule runs, so the failure lands with current_rule unset.\n\nFixture: `LIST X = \"[\" ; DELIMITERS = VSTR:/{X}/r ;` — {X} expands to \"[\", giving the regex \"[\", an unterminated character class. Verified output names the input:\n  ERROR input.txt: cannot construct tag `/\"[\"/r` (cannot compile regex ...), on line 3\n\nNote the premise also needed correcting: the live path no longer emits the string \"RT INPUT\" at all — error_at now yields cfg.input_name (core.rs:2091). The literal label survives only in the error/error_s/error_ss family (core.rs:1474-1482), which is the faithful port of the C++ error() whose emission is deferred and which has zero production callers; tests/engine.rs:304-306 pins it deliberately as an unreached function. That family is not this node's business and is left alone.\n\nFiled separately as stale-current-rule-label: current_rule is never cleared once set, so the input branch is only reachable BEFORE the first window runs, and every stream-reading failure after that is mislabelled against a finished rule. The fix widens this branch rather than retiring it, which is the opposite of what this node was opened to consider."
                    }
                )
            }
        )
    }

