    {
        id ctx-override-restore-on-error
        title "Restore the contextual-test overrides on the error path: both save/restore families return through a ? between the save and the restore, leaving a template or dep target permanently overridden for the rest of the run"
        priority 0
        kind @fix
        log (
            {
                at 2026-09-14T13:11:41.036713Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @blocker
                text "Two independent save/restore families over grammar.contexts_arena, both with the restore placed after a fallible call. (1) Matcher::run_contextual_test_tmpl, run_contextual_test.rs:474-521: saves pos/offset/cbarrier/barrier of contexts_arena[tmpl], overrides them, then line 503 is `let mut cohort = self.run_contextual_test(...)?;` with the restore block at 505 AFTER it — the ? skips the restore entirely. (2) Engine::rr_dep_relation, dispatch.rs:1398-1497: saves contexts_arena[dep_target].offset, clamps it per onward-scan iteration, restores at 1497, with three ? sites between (1426, 1452-1453, 1472). Reachable: run_contextual_test's relation branch calls generate_varstring_tag (run_contextual_test.rs:1517) which returns RunError, and add_tag returns RunError::TagConstruction (core.rs:1957) — so a malformed runtime varstring tag triggers it. Severity is higher than it first looks because templates are SHARED: the textual parser assigns the same CtxId to every test naming a template (driver.rs:778), so one skipped restore corrupts that template for every rule referencing it, for the remainder of the run. Fix shape: a guard type with a Drop impl, or restructure so the restore is unconditional; not another early-return. Found while assessing the proposed Arc<GrammarCore>/TagOverlay split — it is a pre-existing bug, not a consequence of that work, and it also happens to be one of the things blocking any future grammar sharing."
            }

            {
                at 2026-09-14T13:16:06.230874Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @status
                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
            }

            {
                at 2026-09-14T13:21:52.326839Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @note
                text "Fixed by restructuring both sites so the restore runs before the failure propagates: run_contextual_test_tmpl holds the result instead of `?`-ing it, restores, then `?`s (the pos_output_helper post-processing stays on the success path, since it reads the match that succeeded); rr_dep_relation wraps its scan loop in a labelled block whose three former `?` sites now `break 'scan Err(e)`, with the offset restore after the block.\n\nNOT covered by a test, and I want that recorded rather than glossed. To reach it you need a RunError raised inside run_contextual_test while a template override is applied, and the only error source there is the varstring expansion of a relation tag (run_contextual_test.rs:1527). I built three grammars aiming at it — a T: template reference with a position prefix, whose template body is a relation test whose relation name is VSTR:$1 expanding to a `(`-leading string (the construct proven to fail in applicator_tag_fallthrough) — and none raised. In each the rule applied normally, which suggests the template test never evaluated the relation branch at all. If that is right, the error path in run_contextual_test_tmpl may be unreachable today and the fix is purely defensive; that would be worth establishing on its own, because it also bounds how much the pre-existing bug ever mattered. Note the severity ceiling either way: RunError propagates uncaught to the CLI, which reports and exits (vislcg3.rs:522), so a corrupted template is only observable to an embedder that catches the error and reuses the applicator.\n\nLeft deliberately unfixed in the same function: on the error path run_contextual_test_tmpl also skips `scratch.tmpl_cntx.linked.pop()` and the min/max/in_template restore. Same defect class, but moving those above the `?` would reorder them relative to pos_output_helper on the SUCCESS path, and I was not willing to perturb matching behaviour to tidy scratch state that the run's termination discards anyway."
            }
        )
    }

    {
        id runtime-tag-index-divergence
        title "Establish whether the reverse indexes going stale for runtime-interned tags matters: rules_by_tag and sets_by_tag are read during a run but never updated for tags interned after load, and num_tags stops tracking the arena"
        priority 3
        kind @chore
        log (
            {
                at 2026-09-14T13:11:52.613735Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @note
                text "Filed as a question, NOT as a confirmed bug — I called it a bug when handing over and then talked myself back out of it, so the node should start from the doubt. Facts: rules_by_tag and sets_by_tag are built in reindex and read during a run (window.rs:328, reflow.rs:772), keyed by tag hash, and are never updated when add_tag_ptr interns a new tag (core.rs:1847-1892). num_tags is set once at parse (driver.rs:783) and diverges from single_tags_list.capacity() during a run. Reason it is probably harmless: add_tag DEDUPS on text, so an id that is genuinely newly allocated is text no grammar rule or set mentions by name — hence it correctly has no reverse-index entry. Tags whose text DOES appear in the grammar resolve to the existing id, which already carries its entries. Regex/icase matching against runtime tags is handled by the separate T_TEXTUAL rescan (core.rs:1993-2037), not by these indexes. What would falsify that: any path that allocates a new id for text already present (seed-probe collision behaviour is worth reading closely), or any consumer of num_tags on a grammar that has been run. If both check out, resolve this by documenting the invariant on the index fields — the divergence is real even if benign, and it will be re-discovered by the next person reading add_tag_ptr."
            }

            {
                at 2026-09-14T13:33:30.4103Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @status
                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
            }

            {
                at 2026-09-14T13:35:19.287955Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @decision
                text "BENIGN BY CONSTRUCTION — not a defect. Resolved by writing the invariant down and pinning the fact it rests on, rather than by changing behaviour.\n\nMy filing reason (add_tag dedups on text, so a newly allocated id is text nothing names) survives, but it was only half the argument and the missing half is the load-bearing one. Two legs:\n\n1. Dedup is TOTAL, by an open-addressing argument, not merely usual. `single_tags` is never erased from — destroy_tag explicitly does not unregister and has no production caller — so the hash+seed probe chain is prefix-stable. If a grammar tag landed at hash+k, keys hash..hash+k-1 were occupied at that moment by other text and still are, so the runtime probe replays the same chain and finds it. A hash collision between different texts does not break this; that is what the seed is for.\n\n2. The residual case my reason ignored: the runtime tag's BASE hash must equal the grammar tag's. Tag::rehash folds eight type bits into the hash, and the closing move is that all eight are members of MASK_TAG_SPECIAL — verified by reading both lists. So any tag whose hash can diverge is T_SPECIAL, its containing set is ST_SPECIAL, and index_sets files it under tag_any without descending, where it is reached via sets_any rather than a per-tag key. The gap is covered by construction, not left empty.\n\nnum_tags turned out to be the opposite of a wart: its staleness is REQUIRED. write_binary_grammar writes the count and then emits 0..num_tags, so it must remain the parse-time count — runtime tags appear in no trie and must not be serialised. Reachable after a run via `vislcg3 --grammar-bin` without --grammar-only. Confirmed as C++ parity by BinaryGrammar_write.md. rules_any/sets_any cannot stale either: they snapshot tag_any entries that only reindex writes, and every read goes through insert_if_exists, which only grows the destination.\n\nPinned by tag_interning_closure (tests/model_core.rs), which asserts the rehash/MASK_TAG_SPECIAL coupling directly for each of the eight bits. Nothing else in the suite would catch a new hash-contributing bit added outside the mask, and the failure mode would be a silent false negative in possible_sets gating. Two things would turn this into a real defect and are named in the field docs: giving destroy_tag a production caller (which punches a hole in the probe chain AND breaks the \"runtime tags sit above num_tags\" invariant the binary writer depends on), or adding a hash-contributing bit outside MASK_TAG_SPECIAL."
            }
        )
    }

    {
        id stale-current-rule-label
        title "Stop the runtime error label naming a rule that finished: current_rule is set when a rule runs and never cleared, so every stream-reading failure after the first window is attributed to whichever rule happened to run last"
        priority 2
        kind @fix
        log (
            {
                at 2026-09-14T13:32:12.567075Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @note
                text "scratch.current_rule is initialised None (grammar_applicator/mod.rs:653), set at run_rules/schedule.rs:77, saved/restored around sub-rules at dispatch.rs:621/638 — and never set back to None when rules finish. Verified: no assignment of None exists outside the initialiser. So `None` means \"no rule has run yet in this process\", not \"no rule is running\".\n\nThe consequence is a lying label. Stream reading and rule running interleave — the reader buffers windows, runs them, reads more — and the delimiter set is tested against each cohort as it is read (run_grammar.rs:667/693/736, plus the same shape in every other stream applicator). A varstring delimiter that fails during a read AFTER the first window has run is reported as RT RULE against whichever rule finished last, which has nothing to do with it. That directly undercuts [spec:cg3:req:diagnostics.runtime-input-named]: the input branch is reachable (pinned by runtime_input_failure_names_the_input) but only in the window before any rule has run, and the rule branch is wrong everywhere after.\n\nDo NOT just assign None at the end of run_rules without reading match_set.rs:1178-1183 first. current_rule is not only a diagnostic there — it is read to pull RF_CAPTURE_UNIF off the current rule, feeding `cap_unif` and hence whether unification tags/sets are loaded into the match. That read is gated on `context.is_some()`, and delimiter checks pass None, so clearing it looks safe for the diagnostic case — but \"looks safe\" is exactly the claim that needs a test rather than an argument, and this is matching behaviour, not labelling.\n\nAlso worth settling as part of this: the fix WIDENS the input branch rather than narrowing it, so the existing CLI test only covers the easy case. A second fixture where the failure lands after a rule has run would be the real regression test, and constructing one probably needs a varstring delimiter that resolves through a variable a rule sets."
            }

            {
                at 2026-09-14T13:36:30.522882Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @status
                text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
            }

            {
                at 2026-09-14T13:39:40.325149Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @note
                text "Cleared at the end of run_rules_on_single_window (schedule.rs), not on the `?` path above it: a failure raised while a rule is running belongs to that rule, which is the one case the RT RULE label already gets right.\n\nThe risk flagged when filing is resolved, by inspection rather than by the suite passing. current_rule is read non-diagnostically at match_set.rs:1178-1183 to pull RF_CAPTURE_UNIF off the current rule, and clearing it would flip cap_unif to false — which, because the guard reads `!cap_unif`, would turn the unification-tag load ON where it had been off. That would be a real matching change. It cannot fire here: the branch is gated on `context.is_some()`, and every Some(context) caller is in run_contextual_test.rs (:242, :245, :285), i.e. contextual-test evaluation, which only runs while a rule is in flight. Every other caller — all eight stream applicators' delimiter checks — passes None. Conformance suites agree (225/229 green either side), but the argument is what settles it.\n\nPinned by current_rule_does_not_outlive_the_run (tests/engine.rs), asserting the state after a completed run rather than through a diagnostic. Reaching the mislabel through an actual error needs a failure that fires only mid-read on a later window, which means a delimiter varstring resolving through a variable a rule sets — a far more elaborate fixture than the property warrants. The label itself is already pinned end-to-end by tools_cli::runtime_input_failure_names_the_input."
            }
        )
    }

