                        kind @decision
                        text "The sixteen-name list was produced by a regex that stops at the first uppercase letter, so four entries are prefixes of C++ camelCase spellings (ux_str/ux_strip/ux_is = ux_strCaseCompare/ux_stripBOM/ux_isSetOp) and four more (ux_err, ux_stderr, ux_stdin, ux_stdout) exist only in comments naming C++ members. The actual Rust surface was eight public fns, four private duplicates, one differently-shaped private copy and two locals. Renamed: ux_strip_bom -> strip_bom, ux_dirname -> dir_prefix, ux_is_set_op -> set_op_code, ux_is_empty -> is_blank, ux_simplecasecmp -> matches_keyword_chars, ux_simplecasecmp_sv -> matches_keyword, ux_str_case_compare -> eq_ignore_case, ux_bufcpy -> copy_with_visible_newlines, plus locals ux_stdout -> out_stream and ux_stdin_file -> stdin_file in vislcg3. No rule retired: all four deleted eq_ignore_case copies were deliberately un-annotated stand-ins, so the id stayed on the surviving public one, and the HashUString sibling overload is marked \"not separately specced\" in the sem rule, so dropping call_view orphaned nothing. math_parser's ux_simplecasecmp turned out to be a duplicate of ux_simplecasecmp_sv rather than of the fn it shares a name with, and provably so: both tails end in an always-true `combining_class(c) == 0`, so the isspace/isdelim difference is unreachable. Collapsing it gave the shared &str helper the production caller it lacked and took math_parser's local combining_class with it, leaving its local stand-ins as exactly the four inlines.hpp char predicates. Kept uextras.rs: module paths in this crate map 1:1 onto the C++ translation unit they port and key the spec ids, and unlike icu_uoptions the `u` is inside cg3's own file name rather than a claim about a dependency. 224 tests (228 with profiler), coverage 812/812, cg-comp byte-identical on all 68 grammars."
