    {
        id grammar-split
        title "Make a loaded grammar shareable: split it into an immutable Arc core and a per-run overlay, so a process running N pipelines holds one copy of the sets, rules, contexts and tags instead of N"
        kind @perf
        children (
            {
                id grammar-split.transient-writes
                title "Rehome the three writes that were never grammar state: the template and dep-target overrides belong to the test being evaluated, the failing-test reorder belongs to the run that observed it, and has_relations is a stream property the converter discovers"
                priority 0
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-09-14T21:04:50.841431Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-09-14T21:29:21.523282Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @decision
                        text "All four writes rehomed; behaviour preserved byte-for-byte. Mechanism notes and the two judgement calls:\n\n(1)+(2) One mechanism for both position overrides: `contextual_test::TestRef` — a `CtxId` plus an optional `TestOverride` carrying the effective pos/offset/barrier/cbarrier. Every `test: CtxId` parameter in the matcher tree became `test: TestRef`, so arity is unchanged and the type system forces every call site to say which it means; the four overridable fields are only reachable through accessors, every other field (`linked`, `tmpl`, `ors`, `target`, `relation`, `hash`, `jump_pos`) is read off `test.id` as before. The iterators carry it too (`CohortIterator::m_test: Option<TestRef>`), because they re-read `test->pos` on every advance and in C++ that read saw the mutated arena. Nesting is preserved by construction: `run_contextual_test_tmpl` builds the inner template's override from `test.pos(c)`/`test.offset(c)` — the OUTER test's EFFECTIVE fields — which is exactly what the C++ reads back out of the arena at that point, and `pos_output_helper` likewise validates against the outer test's effective fields. The `'scan:` labelled block in `rr_dep_relation` is gone: with nothing to restore, `?` propagates directly.\n\nJUDGEMENT (a): `add_contextual_test` deduplicates structurally identical tests into one `CtxId`, so a rule's `dep_test` can BE its `dep_target` (`SETPARENT (V) TO (-2 (N)) (-2 (N))`). C++ clamps the shared object, so such a dep test observes the clamp from iteration 2 onward. Reproduced explicitly (`if it == dep_target { dep_ref }`). A purpose-built stress grammar hits this path, so it is reachable, not theoretical. The mirror case for templates cannot arise: interning is bottom-up, so the reference graph is acyclic and nothing reachable from an overridden test can BE that test.\n\n(3) The failing-test reorder moved to `RuleScratch::test_order` (BTreeMap<rule index, ContextList>), cloned on first reorder. `RuleScratch` is built once in `GrammarApplicator::new` and never replaced, so the order persists for the whole run exactly as the C++ write persists on the grammar it owns — which matters, because test order is observable (the loop breaks at the first failure, and the tests have side effects that feed `POS_JUMP`).\n\nJUDGEMENT (b): `FormatConverter::run_grammar_on_text`'s `has_relations` moved to `EngineConfig::stream_relations`, and BOTH readers were repointed. The runtime one is `reflow.rs` (`grammar.has_relations || cfg.stream_relations`). The second is `binary_grammar`'s `BINF_RELATIONS` bit: because main holds the same grammar the run mutated, `vislcg3 --out-binary --grammar-bin` currently stamps BINF_RELATIONS on a grammar with no relation rules. Measured and confirmed observable (T_Select's emitted .cg3b differs between `--grammar-only --grammar-bin` and a binary-stream run + `--grammar-bin`). Arguably a C++ bug, but preserved rather than fixed: `write_grammar_bin` takes the run's flag and folds it in at the CLI, on the by-value grammar main owns.\n\nVerification: 224/224 (+profiler 228/228), clippy clean both configs, 53 doc warnings (unchanged), cg-comp byte-identity 68 identical / 0 differing / 0 skipped, plus 204 old-vs-new vislcg3 runs (68 grammars x plain/--trace/--dep-delimit) and a nested-template stress grammar (3569 nested overrides, the dep-test-aliases-clamped-target case) all identical."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-09-14T21:33:08.687776Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id grammar-split.flags-to-overlay
                title "Move tag type flags out of Tag into a parallel array the run owns: four of the runtime write families exist only because a mutable u32 sits inside an otherwise immutable record, and a dense array is both per-run by construction and the fastest layout for the hottest read in the engine"
                priority 0
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-09-14T21:44:12.371241Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-09-14T22:17:39.303193Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @decision
                        text "Tag type flags are now the RUN's, in a dense array beside the tag arena. `Tag::r#type` stays put and keeps the load-time value, exactly as planned — the 39 sites inside tag.rs, the hash/dedup contract `rehash` folds eight type bits into, and the binary writer all read it unchanged.\n\nSHAPE. `Grammar::tag_flags: Vec<TagType>`, parallel to `single_tags_list` (same index, same length). Three accessors on `Grammar`: `tag_type(TagId) -> TagType`, `tag_type_insert(TagId, bits)`, `tag_type_remove(TagId, bits)`. They are the only code that knows where the array lives, so phase 3 relocates one field and edits three one-line bodies rather than ~130 call sites.\n\nMATERIALISATION, and how I convinced myself the point is right. Two mechanisms, deliberately overlapping. (a) `Grammar::intern_tag_slot` — a new single choke point for \"a Tag enters the arena\" — stamps `number` and seeds the new slot's flags. There were THREE interners (`Grammar::add_tag`, `tag.rs`'s `R:` relation interner, and the applicator's own `add_tag_ptr`), two of which run mid-stream; all three route through it now, so the array is never short of the arena. (b) `Grammar::materialise_tag_flags` re-seeds wholesale as a new final step of `reindex`.\n\n(b) is needed because the binary loader writes arena slots directly (`single_tags_list[number] = t`), bypassing every interner, and because `reindex` ITSELF is the last writer of `Tag::r#type`: step (5) ORs T_TEXTUAL from the regex/icase scan, step (6) marks T_USED, step (11) recomputes T_MAPPING over the whole arena. Placing the copy after step (20) absorbs all three.\n\nNothing mutates an arena tag's flags after that. Checked by exhaustion rather than by reading the happy path: across the crate exactly two statements write a type bit on a tag that lives in the arena — `grammar.rs`'s step-(5) T_TEXTUAL and `tag_trie.rs`'s `trie_mark_used` T_USED — plus step (11)'s `get_mut` pair and three `mark_used()` calls, all lexically inside `reindex`; `trie_mark_used` is reachable only from `Set::mark_used`, whose every caller is also inside `reindex`. Every other write in the crate is to a LOCAL `Tag` before interning (`parse_tag`/`parse_tag_raw`, `Tag`'s own methods, the two `tagcopy` failfast rewrites) or is a Set/Rule/Cohort type. The four named hazards check out: `set_grammar` interns its magic tags through `add_tag`, so (a) covers them; `mwesplit_applicator` and `format_converter` reindex the grammar they build in place before running it; `cg-relabel` interns through `Grammar::add_tag` and ends `relabel()` with a reindex. Tag arena allocation has four sites, and the one that bypasses the interner (the binary loader's bulk pre-size) is always followed by a reindex.\n\nWRITES CONVERTED, all to the array and only the array: core.rs:2004/2030 (`|= T_TEXTUAL`, the pair that ORs across the ENTIRE arena and so wrote to grammar-compiled tags); run_grammar.rs:358/381 and reflow.rs:811 (`|= T_MAPPING` on ids `add_tag` may have deduped onto a pre-existing tag); binary_applicator.rs:282/284 (set/CLEAR per window, the only site that clears). Their guards moved too — the `continue` that skips already-textual tags now reads the run's flags, as the C++ read of the live object did.\n\nREADS. Every runtime read goes through `tag_type`. Where a helper took a bare `&Tag` cloned out of the arena and could not name it, the id is threaded alongside: `does_tag_match_reading`, `does_regexp_match_reading`, `generate_varstring_tag`, and the free fn `test_tag_numerical`. Every call site already had the id in hand. `does_tag_match_reading` reads the flags ONCE at the top rather than per arm: the arms are a single else-if chain whose conditions are pure, and only a taken arm reaches code that can intern, by which point the chain is over. Load-time code — textual_parser, binary_grammar, the reindex, relabeller, grammar_writer, Tag's own methods — still reads `Tag::r#type` directly, so the binary writer keeps serialising load-time flags and the has_relations quirk preserved in the previous phase is untouched.\n\nVERIFICATION. 225 tests (224 + one new), 0 failures; 229 with `--features profiler`. clippy clean both configs. cargo doc 53 warnings, unchanged, none naming the new symbols. nplan lint/spec-stale/spec-validate clean, coverage 812/812.\n\ncg-comp byte-identity against the start commit built in a detached worktree: 68 identical, 0 differing, 0 skipped, binaries hashing differently.\n\nBehavioural equivalence: 204 old-vs-new `vislcg3` fixture runs (68 grammars x plain/--trace/--dep-delimit, stdout and stderr) all identical, plus 68 binary-stream round-trips (--out-binary -> --in-binary --out-binary -> --out-cg) of which 67 are byte-identical at both binary stages and T_External fails identically on both binaries at the same stage, needing an external helper process the harness does not provide (golden.rs excludes it for the same reason).\n\nJUDGEMENT: the fixture corpus does not reach the runtime T_TEXTUAL rescan at all — instrumenting the write sites and running all 68 fixtures counted 2085 reflow T_MAPPING writes, 118 stream T_MAPPING writes and 65/1665 binary set/clear, but ZERO T_TEXTUAL. The reason is that the rescan needs `regex_tags` to gain a member mid-run, and every runtime-generated regex in the corpus expands to a QUOTED pattern, which `is_textual` accepts and the branch therefore skips. A purpose-built stress grammar generates SLASH-delimited patterns (`VSTR:/gen-$1$2-.*/r`) absent from the grammar, which do reach it: 4 rescan writes, output identical across old and new on all four flag variants. Byte-identity alone would not have covered this write family, which is the one the whole phase exists for.\n\nA test pins the property directly rather than a consequence of it: `engine::runtime_tag_flags_do_not_reach_the_grammar` asserts the array materialises to exactly the load-time flags, that a full run leaves every pre-existing tag's `Tag::r#type` untouched, and that a mapped tag read off the stream is T_MAPPING in the run's flags and NOT in the interned `Tag`."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-09-14T22:17:44.055672Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id grammar-split.core-split
                title "Split Grammar into an immutable GrammarCore behind Arc and a per-run TagOverlay, with tag id resolution reading the core below its length and the overlay above it"
                priority 0
                deps (grammar-split.transient-writes grammar-split.flags-to-overlay)
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-09-14T22:21:15.383154Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-09-14T22:56:28.724476Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @decision
                        text "The grammar is now `GrammarCore` (frozen, `Send + Sync`, `Arc`-able) plus a per-run tag overlay, with `Grammar` the pair and `Deref`/`DerefMut` keeping the whole field surface. 315+ `single_tags_list[id]` reads are untouched.\n\nSHAPE. `GrammarCore` is the old `Grammar` struct minus `tag_flags`, with `single_tags` renamed to `tags_by_hash`. `Grammar` holds five members: `single_tags_list: TagStore` (the spanning arena) and the overlay proper — the run's hash additions, `tag_flags`, `regex_tags`, `icase_tags`. The last three SHADOW core fields of the same name, so `grammar.regex_tags` through a `Grammar` means the run's; `tags_by_hash` is renamed precisely because `single_tags` could not be shadowed (see below) and a field of that name would let a lookup silently see the core half alone.\n\nTHE SPANNING INDEX. `TagStore { core: CoreHandle, run: Vec<Tag> }` implements `Index<u32, Output = Tag>`, `get`, `try_get`, `capacity`: ids below `core.single_tags_list.capacity()` resolve to the core, the rest to `run`. The core arena never grows once frozen, so the boundary is fixed and no core id ever moves. No `IndexMut` — a `&mut Tag` that might land in the core is not expressible. `intern` adds to whichever half is live; `building_mut` / `put_building` / `alloc_building` are the load-time editors and assert on a frozen core (7 production sites: reindex's five `T_TEXTUAL`/`mark_used`/`T_MAPPING` writes, `Set::mark_used`'s ff-tag loop, `trie_markused`, the binary loader's slot-fill and varstring fixup, the parser's `vs_sets`/`vs_names` allocation).\n\nWHY THE HANDLE LIVES IN THE TAG STORE. Loaders mutate the core in place, which needs sole ownership; `Arc::get_mut` fails the moment a second clone exists. `single_tags_list` must reach both halves and Rust fields cannot borrow siblings, so the store has to own the handle — therefore it must be the ONLY owner, and `Grammar::core`/`Deref` read it back out of there. `CoreHandle` is `Building(Box<GrammarCore>)` | `Frozen(Arc<GrammarCore>)`: building mutates with no atomics at all (`reindex` writes millions of times and must not pay a CAS per write), frozen-but-unshared falls back to `Arc::get_mut` and a shared one panics naming the invariant.\n\nCALL SITES TOUCHED. The two arena-shaped surfaces cost 7 (above) + 2 test sites. `single_tags` could not be shadowed without a second `Arc`, so it became `Grammar::single_tags() -> TagIndex<'_>`: ~60 sites gained `()`, mechanically, and the compiler forced every one because the field no longer exists under that name. `TagIndex::find` returns a `TagHashRef` that compares against `end()` and derefs with `.get()`, so the C++ iterator dance at those sites is otherwise unchanged. Four interner `single_tags.insert` sites became `Grammar::insert_tag_hash`. Five `set.set_name(…, &mut g.rand_state)` sites needed one `core_mut()` local (two disjoint core fields cannot both come through `DerefMut`). Everything else — sets, rules, contexts, indexes, scalars — compiled untouched through `Deref`.\n\nDEDUP ACROSS THE SPLIT. `TagIndex::find` consults the core first and the run second, and the two halves hold disjoint keys because an interner only ever inserts a key neither half answers for. So `addTag`'s `hash + seed` walk stops at the first seed nothing answers for, exactly as one map would: a probe over the run alone would find `hash + 0` free and mint a second id for text the core already has. `engine::runtime_interning_dedups_against_the_frozen_core` pins it with a REAL hash collision found by brute force — `\"aac0c\"` and `\"aaepa\"` share a `hash_value_ustring`, so the parser parks the second at `hash + 1`; re-interning that one at runtime only returns the core's id if step 0 saw the core's entry and walked on. The test also checks the un-seeded case, that a genuinely new text lands above the core, that the run dedups against itself, and that neither the core arena nor `num_tags` grew.\n\nFREEZE POINT. `GrammarApplicator::set_grammar`, not the end of a load: `cg-relabel` keeps interning after its last `reindex` and those tags are the GRAMMAR's — they must serialise. Every run path reaches `set_grammar`; a path that does not simply stays unfrozen, where everything goes to the core and behaviour is exactly what it was. `reindex` step (21) (`materialise_run_tag_state`) now seeds the run's `regex_tags`/`icase_tags` from the core's alongside the flags, so an unfrozen grammar reads the same sets it always did.\n\nVERIFICATION. 227 tests (225 + 2 new), 0 failures; 231 with `--features profiler`. clippy clean both configs. cargo doc 53 warnings / 21 unresolved links, both unchanged from the start commit. nplan lint / spec-stale / spec-validate clean, coverage 812/812.\n\ncg-comp byte-identity against the start commit in a detached worktree: 68 identical, 0 differing, 0 skipped, binaries hashing differently. Post-run `vislcg3 --grammar-bin` (the case where a run's tags could leak into a `.cg3b`): 67 identical, 0 differing, T_External skipped for want of its helper process. 204 old-vs-new `vislcg3` fixture runs (68 grammars x plain/--trace/--dep-delimit, comparing the output file, stdout, stderr and exit code) all identical.\n\nPhase 2 found the corpus never reaches the runtime `T_TEXTUAL` rescan, so a purpose-built grammar generating a SLASH-delimited runtime regex (`ADD (VSTR:/gen-$1-.*/r)` against a capturing target, matching BARE core tags — quoted ones are already textual, which is why phase 2's corpus scan found nothing) was run through both binaries on four flag variants: 16 comparisons identical. An in-process probe on it confirms what the phase is for: 12 runtime tags, none in the core arena; the core hash map does not grow; `regex_tags` core 0 / run 3; two CORE tags marked `T_TEXTUAL` in the run's flags with zero `Tag::r#type` rewrites.\n\nIS THE WIN REAL? Measured, not argued: instrumenting `core_mut` to log every post-freeze core mutation and running the whole corpus (68 grammars x 3 flag variants) counted ZERO during a run. The only post-freeze writers are CLI-side and after the run — `write_grammar_bin`/`write_binary_grammar` (623) and the grammar writer's set-naming pass (769) — all on the grammar `main` still solely owns, none reachable from a shared core. The remaining per-run cost is the overlay: a dense `Vec<TagType>` (4 bytes/tag), copies of `regex_tags`/`icase_tags` (a handful of ids), and the run's own tags and hash entries. Nothing in the core is per-run."
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id grammar-split.applicator-arc
                title "Take the shared core at the constructor and thread it through the tools: GrammarApplicator::new accepts an Arc GrammarCore, so a caller loads a grammar once and hands the same one to every pipeline it builds"
                priority 1
                deps (grammar-split.core-split)
            }
        )
        log (
            {
                at 2026-09-14T21:04:22.014769Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @decision
                text "Driven by a measured downstream cost, not tidiness: teaksta rebuilds a cg3 grammar per pipeline handle at ~450 MiB and ~133 ms per worker, which is the entire remaining marginal after its hfst/runtime caches landed, and it caps workers at 4 regardless of cores. Note the tag-slimming work (248→104 bytes) did NOT move it, and could not have: the cost is N copies of the whole grammar, so shrinking each copy divides a number that sharing removes outright.\n\nThe incoming proposal catalogued six runtime write sites. There are eleven. Missing were: core.rs:2004/2030, which OR T_TEXTUAL across the ENTIRE tag arena and therefore write to grammar-compiled tags rather than interned ones (the proposal named the rescan triggers at :1983/:2016 but not the write they cause); reflow.rs:811, a third T_MAPPING back-patch; binary_applicator.rs:284, the only site that CLEARS a flag and does so order-dependently across windows; run_contextual_test.rs:486/506, a second contexts_arena override family beside the dep-offset one; tag.rs:929, a second arena-growth site via the R: relation interner; and format_converter.rs:280 setting has_relations mid-run.\n\nKey design call, which turns the hardest cluster into the easiest: tag type flags go in the OVERLAY, not the core. Four of the eleven families (T_TEXTUAL, all three T_MAPPING writes, and the clear) exist only because a mutable u32 lives inside an otherwise immutable record. A dense per-run Vec<TagType> makes them per-run by construction — no copy-on-write, no delta table consulted at ~270 read sites, and no question of tag identity changing (which would break the hash/dedup contract, since rehash folds eight type bits into the hash). Seeding costs one 4-byte-per-tag memcpy per run: ~56 KB for a 13930-tag grammar against 450 MiB. It is also the fastest layout available — 16 flags per cache line against 0.62 whole tags — so the correct answer and the fast answer coincide.\n\nSequencing keeps the tree building at every step: flags move to a parallel array on Grammar FIRST (phase 2), so phase 3 only has to relocate a field rather than rewrite 270 call sites under a new type. The three transient writes (two override families, the rule reorder) go to RuleScratch in phase 1, independent of everything else.\n\nASSUMPTION, stated because it was asked and not answered: teaksta shares one grammar across workers IN ONE PROCESS. Arc buys nothing across process boundaries; if the workers are separate processes the answer is an mmap'd read-only image instead, and phases 3–4 change shape. Phases 1–2 are worth doing either way. Grammar is already Send + Sync (compile-checked), so the Arc story has no type-level blocker."
            }
        )
    }
)
